diff --git a/evidence/split-bridge-opposite.log b/evidence/split-bridge-opposite.log
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..d920f576672eb103e74bc97ac1ff81cfc3a6f09f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/evidence/split-bridge-opposite.log
@@ -0,0 +1,94 @@
+TARGET EXTENSION:
+/candidate/verification.k:144-152 rewrites
+  #applyK(toCall(boundMethodV(str(sentenceCodes(W)), "split")), .Vals)
+to
+  #alloc(list(wordsVals(W)))
+for validWords(W), at priority 30.
+
+WHY THIS IS RESULT-BEARING:
+`sentenceCodes` is only an opaque constructor. There are no equations relating
+it to W's character sequences or spaces. `wordsVals` is also opaque. The bridge
+is the only connection from the input projection to the word list that drives
+the loop and final result; no bridge-free universal connection theorem exists.
+
+FALSE-CONCLUSION / OPPOSITE-INTERPRETATION WITNESS:
+Let W0 = ["aa", "bbb", "cccc"], which satisfies validWords and has modeled
+sentence length 11. Interpret the otherwise unconstrained
+sentenceCodes(W0) as concrete codes("x"). Then Python and the supplied fixed
+split semantics return ["x"], while the candidate bridge returns W0. The
+conclusion ["aa","bbb","cccc"] = "x".split() is false.
+
+OPPOSITE PROBE A — candidate bridge admits the wrong result:
+The probe adds the ground projection rewrite
+  sentenceCodes(W0) => iCons(120, .IntSeq)
+but leaves the candidate priority-30 bridge in place.
+
+COMMANDS (cwd=/tmp/audit-work/143-opposite):
+kompile verification.k --backend haskell --main-module VERIFICATION \
+  --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX --output-definition opposite-kompiled
+EXIT STATUS: 0
+
+kprove bridge-wrong-spec.k --definition opposite-kompiled \
+  --spec-module BRIDGE-WRONG-SPEC --output pretty
+EXIT STATUS: 0
+OUTPUT: #Top
+
+The proved destination heap is list(wordsVals(W0)), i.e. the wrong W0 split.
+The bridge preempts the nested ground projection rewrite, demonstrating that
